IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Legal Profession Uniform Law (WA) -- the rules a WA lawyer meets once admitted and certified that the Application Act
-- baseline does not already encode: holding myself out as entitled to practise (s 11, with the s 12 presumptions), the
-- categories of practice my practising certificate authorises (s 47), whether my firm may receive trust money (s 150), the
-- statutory conditions on my certificate other than supervision (ss 48, 50, 51, 52, 54) and the General Rules' list of
-- offences I must report (r 15).
--
-- SEAT: the lawyer (Daniel in the WA cradle-to-grave lives; d031, 24 August 2020: "Admitted by the Supreme Court; a practising
-- certificate"). His question, from the plan: "What may I do only once admitted and certified, and what costs agreements may I
-- make?" Every decision is phrased as he would ask it. Where a provision addresses the Legal Practice Board (the "designated
-- local regulatory authority" for Chapter 3 in WA: Application Act s 20), it is encoded as what it means for him. Where the
-- Law hands a judgment to the Board, the answer is conditional on that judgment, which is asked as a fact.
--
-- DIVISION OF GROUND with the Application Act baseline (subject wa/legal-profession-uniform-law-application-act-2022,
-- encoding baseline-2026-10). That encoding already answers, from the Uniform Law: admission (ss 16-19), "Australian lawyer"
-- and the day of admission (Sch 4 cl 11), the grant, renewal and currency of the practising certificate (ss 44, 45, Sch 4 cl 12,
-- General Rules rr 17, 18), "May I engage in legal practice" (ss 6, 10, 43, General Rules r 10), titles (s 12(1), (2),
-- General Rules r 9), supervised legal practice (s 49, General Rules rr 7, 14), and conditional costs agreements, uplift fees and
-- contingency fees (ss 180-185). None of that is repeated here. This encoding takes the rest of what the lawyer meets.
--
-- Provisions covered: [Uniform Law] ss 6 ("qualified entity" (a), (b), (d); "barrister"; "solicitor"; "serious offence"; "tax
-- offence"; "bankruptcy-related event", as a fact), 11, 12(3), (4), 47(1)-(5), 48(2), 50(1), (3), (4), 51(1), (3), 54, 86(a),
-- (b), 88(3), 150; [General Rules] rr 15, 56 (as a fact).
--
-- Edition: [Uniform Law] Legal Profession Uniform Law (WA), legislation.wa.gov.au mrdoc 46913, retrieved 7 September 2026, as
-- deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_46913.txt (the deposit does not show its as-at date).
-- [General Rules] Legal Profession Uniform General Rules 2015, mrdoc 45732, version 00-a0-02, currency from 1 July 2022, as
-- deposited at ../../../legal-profession-uniform-general-rules-2015/registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_45732.txt.
-- A "penalty unit" is "the value of a penalty unit under an Act of Victoria" (s 6); that value is not deposited, so penalties
-- are given in penalty units.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: admission, certificates, s 10, titles, supervision and costs agreements (the Application Act baseline); s 13 (lay
-- associates); foreign lawyers (Part 3.4); the notification of principal place of practice (s 46); discretionary conditions
-- (s 53, General Rules r 16); variation, suspension and cancellation and show cause procedure beyond the notice deadline
-- (Part 3.5); the Continuing Professional Development Rules (not deposited: s 52 is one fact); the barrister's reading program
-- content and length (set by the Board); disqualification (Part 3.9).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the seat of the
-- individual to whom the law is applied, with the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Legal Profession Uniform Law (WA) -- holding out, my certificate's conditions`

§§ `s 6 -- am I a qualified entity`

-- [Uniform Law] s 6: "qualified entity means -- (a) an Australian legal practitioner; or (b) a law practice; or (c) [a foreign
-- lawyer, within Part 3.4]; or (d) an individual engaged in legal practice under the authority of a law of the Commonwealth or
-- of a jurisdiction, other than this Law or the Uniform Rules; or (e) [an entity of a kind specified in the Uniform Rules]".
-- "Australian legal practitioner means an Australian lawyer who holds a current Australian practising certificate".
-- Paragraphs (c) and (e) are not encoded (out of the seat).
@export Am I a qualified entity under the Legal Profession Uniform Law (WA) s 6?
GIVEN `I am an Australian lawyer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been admitted to the Australian legal profession
      `I hold a current practising certificate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you hold a current Australian practising certificate
      `another law authorises my legal practice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a law of the Commonwealth or a State, other than the Uniform Law, authorises you to engage in legal practice
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I a qualified entity` IF
        (`I am an Australian lawyer` AND `I hold a current practising certificate`)
    OR  `another law authorises my legal practice`

§§ `ss 11, 12(3), (4) -- may I say I am entitled to practise`

-- [Uniform Law] s 11(1): "An entity must not advertise or represent, or do anything that states or implies, that it is entitled
-- to engage in legal practice, unless it is a qualified entity. Penalty: 250 penalty units." s 11(2): "A director, partner,
-- officer, employee or agent of an entity must not advertise or represent, or do anything that states or implies, that the
-- entity is entitled to engage in legal practice, unless the entity is a qualified entity. Penalty: 50 penalty units."
-- s 12(3): "The taking or use of a title to which this section applies by a person gives rise to a rebuttable presumption (for
-- the purposes of section 11(1)) that the person represented that he or she is entitled to engage in legal practice." s 12(4):
-- the same for a title used "in connection with an entity", for s 11(2). The titles are in s 12(1): "lawyer, legal practitioner,
-- barrister, solicitor, attorney, counsel or proctor", the silk titles, and any the Uniform Rules add.
@export Am I presumed to have represented that I am entitled to engage in legal practice (Uniform Law s 12(3), (4))?
GIVEN `I used a title listed in s 12(1)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you took or used a title such as lawyer, solicitor, barrister, counsel or legal practitioner
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I presumed to have represented that I am entitled to practise` IF
    `I used a title listed in s 12(1)`

DECLARE `Holding out answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I am a qualified entity`
    `no: it would be an offence under Uniform Law s 11(1), up to 250 penalty units`

@export May I advertise, represent or imply that I am entitled to engage in legal practice (Uniform Law s 11(1))?
GIVEN `I am an Australian lawyer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been admitted to the Australian legal profession
      `I hold a current practising certificate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you hold a current Australian practising certificate
      `another law authorises my legal practice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another law authorises you to engage in legal practice
GIVETH A `Holding out answer`
DECIDE `May I say I am entitled to engage in legal practice` IS
    IF `Am I a qualified entity` `I am an Australian lawyer` `I hold a current practising certificate` `another law authorises my legal practice`
    THEN `yes: I am a qualified entity`
    ELSE `no: it would be an offence under Uniform Law s 11(1), up to 250 penalty units`

DECLARE `Firm holding out answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the entity is a qualified entity`
    `no: it would be an offence under Uniform Law s 11(2), up to 50 penalty units`

-- s 6: a "law practice" is a qualified entity (para (b)).
@export May I, as an employee, partner or agent, represent that my firm is entitled to engage in legal practice (Uniform Law s 11(2))?
GIVEN `the entity is a law practice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the entity is a law practice (for example a law firm of Australian legal practitioners)
      `the entity is otherwise a qualified entity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the entity is otherwise a qualified entity under s 6
GIVETH A `Firm holding out answer`
DECIDE `May I say my firm is entitled to engage in legal practice` IS
    IF `the entity is a law practice` OR `the entity is otherwise a qualified entity`
    THEN `yes: the entity is a qualified entity`
    ELSE `no: it would be an offence under Uniform Law s 11(2), up to 50 penalty units`

§§ `s 6 -- am I a solicitor or a barrister`

-- [Uniform Law] s 6: "barrister means an Australian legal practitioner whose Australian practising certificate is subject to a
-- condition that the holder is authorised to engage in legal practice as or in the manner of a barrister only"; "solicitor means
-- an Australian legal practitioner whose Australian practising certificate is not subject to [that] condition".
DECLARE `Category of practice` IS ONE OF
    `as a principal of a law practice`                          -- s 47(1)(a)(i)
    `as an employee of a law practice`                          -- s 47(1)(a)(ii)
    `as a corporate legal practitioner`                         -- s 47(1)(a)(iii)
    `as a government legal practitioner`                        -- s 47(1)(a)(iv)
    `as or in the manner of a barrister only`                   -- s 47(1)(b)
    `as a community legal service volunteer and pro bono only`  -- s 47(1)(c)
    `as a volunteer or otherwise on a pro bono basis`           -- s 47(5): a way of practising, not a category the Board grants

DECLARE `Branch of the profession` IS ONE OF
    `a solicitor`
    `a barrister`
    `neither: I am not an Australian legal practitioner`

@export Am I a solicitor or a barrister under the Legal Profession Uniform Law (WA) s 6?
GIVEN `I am an Australian legal practitioner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an Australian lawyer with a current practising certificate
      `my certificate's category` IS A `Category of practice` @desc The category of practice your certificate authorises
GIVETH A `Branch of the profession`
DECIDE `Am I a solicitor or a barrister` IS
    IF (NOT `I am an Australian legal practitioner`)
    THEN `neither: I am not an Australian legal practitioner`
    ELSE IF `my certificate's category` EQUALS `as or in the manner of a barrister only`
         THEN `a barrister`
         ELSE `a solicitor`

§§ `s 47 -- in what capacity may I practise`

-- [Uniform Law] s 47(1): a certificate "is subject to the condition, as determined by the designated local regulatory authority,
-- that the holder is authorised to engage in legal practice -- (a) in one or more of the following categories -- (i) as a
-- principal of a law practice; (ii) as an employee of a law practice; (iii) as a corporate legal practitioner; (iv) as a
-- government legal practitioner; or (b) as or in the manner of a barrister only; or (c) both as a volunteer at a community legal
-- service and otherwise on a pro bono basis, only."
-- s 47(3): a principal certificate "also authorises the holder to engage in legal practice as an employee of a law practice or a
-- corporate legal practitioner or government legal practitioner".
-- s 47(4): "Until the Australian practising certificate concerned is renewed, and subject to any relevant conditions -- (a)
-- [employee] also authorises ... corporate legal practitioner or government legal practitioner; and (b) [corporate] also
-- authorises ... government legal practitioner; and (c) [government] also authorises ... corporate legal practitioner."
-- s 47(5): a certificate under (1)(a) or (b) "also authorises the holder to engage in legal practice as a volunteer at a community
-- legal service, or otherwise on a pro bono basis" (the Note: discretionary conditions may restrict this).
-- One case is one category. A certificate in several (a) categories is asked once per category.
@export Does my practising certificate let me practise in this capacity (Uniform Law s 47(1), (3)-(5))?
GIVEN `my certificate's category` IS A `Category of practice` @desc The category the Board determined for your certificate
      `the capacity I want to practise in` IS A `Category of practice` @desc The capacity you want to practise in
      `my certificate has been renewed since that category was determined` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your certificate has been renewed since (s 47(4) lasts only until renewal)
      `a discretionary condition restricts my pro bono work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a s 53 discretionary condition prohibits or restricts your pro bono or community legal service work
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does my certificate let me practise as` IF
        `the capacity I want to practise in` EQUALS `my certificate's category`
    OR  CONSIDER `the capacity I want to practise in`
        WHEN `as a volunteer or otherwise on a pro bono basis` THEN
                (NOT `a discretionary condition restricts my pro bono work`)
            AND (NOT (`my certificate's category` EQUALS `as a volunteer or otherwise on a pro bono basis`))
        WHEN `as an employee of a law practice` THEN
                `my certificate's category` EQUALS `as a principal of a law practice`
        WHEN `as a corporate legal practitioner` THEN
                `my certificate's category` EQUALS `as a principal of a law practice`
            OR  (    (NOT `my certificate has been renewed since that category was determined`)
                 AND (   `my certificate's category` EQUALS `as an employee of a law practice`
                      OR `my certificate's category` EQUALS `as a government legal practitioner`))
        WHEN `as a government legal practitioner` THEN
                `my certificate's category` EQUALS `as a principal of a law practice`
            OR  (    (NOT `my certificate has been renewed since that category was determined`)
                 AND (   `my certificate's category` EQUALS `as an employee of a law practice`
                      OR `my certificate's category` EQUALS `as a corporate legal practitioner`))
        OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `ss 47(2), 150, General Rules r 56 -- may my firm receive trust money`

-- [Uniform Law] s 47(2): a certificate "is subject to a condition, as determined by the designated local regulatory authority,
-- that the holder is authorised or not authorised to receive trust money". s 150: "A law practice must not receive trust money
-- unless -- (a) a principal of the law practice holds an Australian practising certificate authorising the receipt of trust
-- money; or (b) the law practice is otherwise authorised to receive trust money under the Uniform Rules. Civil penalty: 250
-- penalty units." [General Rules] r 56: an incorporated legal practice with no such principal may be authorised through a person
-- appointed under Uniform Law s 106(5) -- one fact. The authority turns on a PRINCIPAL's certificate: an employee's own trust
-- authorisation does not authorise the firm.
@export May my firm receive trust money (Uniform Law s 150, General Rules r 56)?
GIVEN `a principal holds a certificate authorising receipt of trust money` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a principal (for example a partner) of your firm holds a practising certificate authorising the receipt of trust money
      `the firm is otherwise authorised under the Uniform Rules` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the firm is otherwise authorised under the Uniform Rules (General Rules r 56)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May my firm receive trust money` IF
    `a principal holds a certificate authorising receipt of trust money` OR `the firm is otherwise authorised under the Uniform Rules`

§§ `ss 48(2), 50 -- one certificate; the barrister's reading program`

-- [Uniform Law] s 48(2): "the holder must not apply for or hold another Australian practising certificate that would be in force
-- concurrently with the first certificate".
@export May I apply for another practising certificate (Uniform Law s 48(2))?
GIVEN `it would be in force at the same time as my current certificate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the new certificate would be in force at the same time as the one you hold
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I apply for another practising certificate` IF
    NOT `it would be in force at the same time as my current certificate`

-- s 50(1): a barrister-only certificate carries the condition that the holder "must -- (a) undertake and complete to the
-- satisfaction of the designated local regulatory authority a reading program ... and (b) read for a period specified by the
-- designated local regulatory authority with a barrister". s 50(3): "once satisfied, does not have to be complied with again
-- unless the designated local regulatory authority otherwise directs". s 50(4): the Board "may exempt a person".
@export Must I complete a barrister's reading program (Uniform Law s 50)?
GIVEN `my certificate's category` IS A `Category of practice` @desc The category of practice your certificate authorises
      `I have satisfied the reading condition before` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already completed the reading program and reading period
      `the Board directs me to do it again` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Board has directed you to comply again
      `the Board has exempted me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Board has exempted you
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I complete a reading program` IF
        `my certificate's category` EQUALS `as or in the manner of a barrister only`
    AND (NOT `the Board has exempted me`)
    AND ((NOT `I have satisfied the reading condition before`) OR `the Board directs me to do it again`)

§§ `ss 51, 86, 88, General Rules r 15 -- what I must report, and by when`

-- [Uniform Law] s 51(1): the holder "must notify the designated local regulatory authority in writing within 7 days that -- (a)
-- the holder has been charged with or convicted of a serious offence, a tax offence or an offence specified in the Uniform Rules
-- ...; or (b) a bankruptcy-related event has occurred in relation to the holder; or (c) the holder has become the subject of
-- disciplinary proceedings as a lawyer in a foreign country." s 51(3): "Subsection (1) does not apply to an offence to which
-- section 86 applies."
-- s 86: an "automatic show cause event" is "(a) a bankruptcy-related event; (b) his or her conviction for a serious offence or a
-- tax offence". s 88(3): the holder must give "(a) the notice within 7 days after the show cause event occurred; and (b) the
-- statement within 28 days" (explaining why he is still fit and proper).
-- So a CHARGE for a serious or tax offence is reported under s 51; a CONVICTION for one is not (s 51(3)) but is an automatic
-- show cause event under s 88. A bankruptcy-related event is reported under both: s 51(3) lifts only offences.
-- s 6: "serious offence means an offence that is -- (a) an indictable offence against a law of the Commonwealth, a State or a
-- Territory (whether or not the offence is or may be dealt with summarily)"; "tax offence means any offence under the Taxation
-- Administration Act 1953 of the Commonwealth".
-- [General Rules] r 15(2): "Summary offences are prescribed ... but only in relation to the requirement to notify a conviction."
-- r 15(3): except summary transport or traffic offences "(a) relating to the parking of vehicles, or (b) relating to the driving
-- or operation of vehicles, unless the offence concerned is a notifiable summary offence". r 15(4): a notifiable summary offence
-- is one "(a) where the court has sentenced the offender to a term of imprisonment, (b) ... that has a maximum penalty of
-- imprisonment of 6 months or more, (c) ... where the court has ordered licence disqualification on conviction, (d) [drink or
-- drug driving] under -- (i) the Road Transport Act 2013 of New South Wales, or (ii) the Road Safety Act 1986 of Victoria".
-- r 15(5): "summary offence means an offence ... other than a serious offence or a tax offence".
-- Paragraph (d) names only New South Wales and Victorian Acts. A WA drink-driving conviction is notifiable only through (a), (b)
-- or (c).
DECLARE `What happened` IS ONE OF
    `I was charged with an offence`
    `I was convicted of an offence`
    `a bankruptcy-related event`
    `I became subject to disciplinary proceedings as a lawyer in a foreign country`
    `nothing reportable`

DECLARE `Offence class` IS ONE OF
    `an indictable offence`                                -- s 6 "serious offence" (a)
    `an offence under the Taxation Administration Act 1953` -- s 6 "tax offence"
    `a summary offence`                                    -- General Rules r 15(5)

DECLARE `The offence` HAS
    `class`                                                  IS AN `Offence class`
    `under transport or traffic legislation`                 IS A BOOLEAN  -- r 15(3)
    `about parking`                                          IS A BOOLEAN  -- r 15(3)(a)
    `about driving or operating a vehicle`                   IS A BOOLEAN  -- r 15(3)(b)
    `I was sentenced to imprisonment`                        IS A BOOLEAN  -- r 15(4)(a)
    `maximum imprisonment in months`                         IS A NUMBER   -- r 15(4)(b)
    `licence disqualification ordered`                       IS A BOOLEAN  -- r 15(4)(c)
    `drink or drug driving under the NSW or Victorian Act`   IS A BOOLEAN  -- r 15(4)(d)

GIVEN `the offence` IS A `The offence`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a notifiable summary offence` MEANS
        `the offence`'s `I was sentenced to imprisonment`
    OR  `the offence`'s `maximum imprisonment in months` AT LEAST 6
    OR  `the offence`'s `licence disqualification ordered`
    OR  `the offence`'s `drink or drug driving under the NSW or Victorian Act`

-- General Rules r 15(2)-(4): is a conviction for this summary offence a prescribed offence for s 51(1)(a)?
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `The offence`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a prescribed summary conviction` MEANS
        `the offence`'s `class` EQUALS `a summary offence`
    AND (   (NOT `the offence`'s `under transport or traffic legislation`)
         OR (    (NOT `the offence`'s `about parking`)
             AND (NOT `the offence`'s `about driving or operating a vehicle`))
         OR (    (NOT `the offence`'s `about parking`)
             AND `a notifiable summary offence` `the offence`))

DECLARE `Reporting answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: notify the Board in writing within 7 days (s 51(1))`
    `yes: notify the Board in writing within 7 days (s 51(1)); it is also an automatic show cause event, so give a written notice within 7 days and a statement within 28 days (s 88)`
    `not under s 51: it is an automatic show cause event, so give the Board a written notice within 7 days and a statement within 28 days (s 88)`
    `no: neither s 51 nor s 88 requires it`

@export Must I report this to the Legal Practice Board, and how (Uniform Law ss 51, 86, 88, General Rules r 15)?
GIVEN `what happened` IS A `What happened` @desc What happened to you
      `the offence` IS A `The offence` @desc The offence, if what happened was a charge or a conviction
GIVETH A `Reporting answer`
DECIDE `Must I report this to the Board` IS
    CONSIDER `what happened`
    WHEN `a bankruptcy-related event` THEN
        `yes: notify the Board in writing within 7 days (s 51(1)); it is also an automatic show cause event, so give a written notice within 7 days and a statement within 28 days (s 88)`
    WHEN `I became subject to disciplinary proceedings as a lawyer in a foreign country` THEN
        `yes: notify the Board in writing within 7 days (s 51(1))`
    WHEN `I was charged with an offence` THEN
        IF `the offence`'s `class` EQUALS `a summary offence`
        THEN `no: neither s 51 nor s 88 requires it`
        ELSE `yes: notify the Board in writing within 7 days (s 51(1))`
    WHEN `I was convicted of an offence` THEN
        IF `the offence`'s `class` EQUALS `a summary offence`
        THEN IF `a prescribed summary conviction` `the offence`
             THEN `yes: notify the Board in writing within 7 days (s 51(1))`
             ELSE `no: neither s 51 nor s 88 requires it`
        ELSE `not under s 51: it is an automatic show cause event, so give the Board a written notice within 7 days and a statement within 28 days (s 88)`
    OTHERWISE `no: neither s 51 nor s 88 requires it`

-- "within 7 days" (s 51(1), s 88(3)(a)). Counted here as calendar days after the event; the Law's "business day" is not used
-- by these provisions.
@export Is my written notice to the Board in time (Uniform Law ss 51(1), 88(3)(a): within 7 days)?
GIVEN `the date it happened` IS A DATE @desc The date of the charge, conviction or other event
      `the date I notify` IS A DATE @desc The date you give the Board written notice
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my notice in time` IF
    Day `the date I notify` AT MOST Day `the date it happened` PLUS 7

§§ `ss 52, 54 -- complying with my certificate's conditions`

-- [Uniform Law] s 52: "the holder must comply with the applicable requirements of the Continuing Professional Development Rules"
-- (not deposited). s 54: "The holder of an Australian practising certificate granted in this jurisdiction must comply with the
-- conditions of the certificate. Civil penalty: 100 penalty units."
@export What is the most I can be ordered to pay for breaching a condition of my practising certificate (Uniform Law s 54), in penalty units?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be ordered to pay for breaching a condition` IS 100
